4c:["$","div",null,{"className":"bg-gray-50 p-6 rounded","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"text-xl font-bold text-notary-navy mb-3","children":"Should I buy an all-in-one printer with scanning?"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-gray-700","children":"Only if you need to scan regularly (scan-backs, copying borrower IDs, etc.). All-in-ones cost more, have more parts that can break, and are slower. If you just need printing, get a print-only model. If you need scanning, the Brother MFC-L2900DW (~$330) is the best budget all-in-one."}]]}]
4d:["$","div",null,{"className":"bg-gray-50 p-6 rounded","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"text-xl font-bold text-notary-navy mb-3","children":"How long do laser printers last?"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-gray-700","children":"A well-maintained laser printer should last 5-10 years. Brother printers are especially durable - many notaries report using the same Brother printer for 7+ years. Key to longevity: use OEM toner, clean regularly, and don't exceed the monthly duty cycle."}]]}]
4e:["$","div",null,{"className":"bg-gray-50 p-6 rounded","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"text-xl font-bold text-notary-navy mb-3","children":"What paper should I use for loan documents?"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-gray-700","children":"Use 20-24lb bright white copy paper from reputable brands (Hammermill, Georgia-Pacific, Staples brand). Avoid ultra-cheap paper - it causes jams and looks unprofessional. Cost: ~$30-40 per case (2,500 sheets), which is about 300-400 loan packages."}]]}]
4f:["$","div",null,{"className":"bg-gray-50 p-6 rounded","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"text-xl font-bold text-notary-navy mb-3","children":"Can I use third-party toner cartridges?"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-gray-700","children":"Not recommended. Third-party toner causes print quality issues, paper jams, and can void your warranty. Stick with OEM (Brother, HP) toner. The quality difference is significant, and OEM toner lasts longer, so the real cost difference is smaller than it appears."}]]}]
50:["$","div",null,{"className":"bg-gray-50 p-6 rounded","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"text-xl font-bold text-notary-navy mb-3","children":"Do I need a color printer for notary work?"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-gray-700","children":"No. 99.9% of loan documents are black and white. Color laser printers cost 2-3x more and are slower. Save your money and get a monochrome (black and white) laser printer. If you need occasional color printing, use a local print shop or a cheap inkjet for those rare occasions."}]]}]
51:["$","div",null,{"className":"bg-gray-50 p-6 rounded","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"text-xl font-bold text-notary-navy mb-3","children":"What print speed do I need for loan signings?"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-gray-700","children":"Minimum 30 ppm, ideally 40+ ppm. At 40 ppm, a 200-page package takes 5 minutes. At 30 ppm, it takes 6.7 minutes. At 15 ppm (typical inkjet), it takes 13+ minutes. The faster the printer, the more signings you can fit into your schedule."}]]}]
52:["$","div",null,{"className":"bg-gray-50 p-6 rounded","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"text-xl font-bold text-notary-navy mb-3","children":"What's the monthly duty cycle and why does it matter?"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-gray-700","children":"Duty cycle is the maximum number of pages per month the printer can handle without damage. A printer rated for 15,000 pages/month will break if you consistently print 20,000 pages/month. Match the duty cycle to your volume: part-time notaries need 15,000+, full-time loan signing agents need 50,000+."}]]}]
53:["$","div",null,{"className":"bg-gray-50 p-6 rounded","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"text-xl font-bold text-notary-navy mb-3","children":"Should I get an HP or Brother printer?"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-gray-700","children":"Both make quality printers. Brother dominates the notary market because their dual-tray models are more affordable and notaries trust them. HP makes good printers too, especially their enterprise line, but they're typically more expensive for similar specs. For most notaries, Brother offers better value."}]]}]
54:["$","div",null,{"className":"bg-gray-50 p-6 rounded","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"text-xl font-bold text-notary-navy mb-3","children":"What happens if my printer breaks during a busy week?"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-gray-700","children":"Always have a backup plan: (1) Keep a second printer (even a cheap one), (2) Know where your nearest FedEx/UPS store is (they print for $0.10-0.15/page), or (3) Have a notary friend nearby you can call in emergencies. Don't let a printer failure cost you $500+ in missed signings."}]]}]
